Alright — let’s get this straight. You’ve got a basic **DHT11 sensor setup**, and now you want to **turn an LED on or off** based on specific **temperature and humidity thresholds**.  

Here’s a **fixed and working version** of your code with that feature added (and also corrected syntax issues — your original had several typos like `Serial.printIn` instead of `Serial.println`).  

---

### ✅ **Fixed + LED-Control Version**
```cpp
#include <DHT.h>

#define DHTPIN 2          // DHT11 data pin connected to digital pin 2
#define DHTTYPE DHT11     // Define the sensor type
#define LEDPIN 13         // LED connected to digital pin 13

DHT dht(DHTPIN, DHTTYPE);

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
  Serial.println("DHT11 Test Starting...");
  dht.begin();
  pinMode(LEDPIN, OUTPUT);  // set LED pin as output
}

void loop() {
  delay(2000); // wait 2 seconds between readings

  float h = dht.readHumidity();
  float t = dht.readTemperature();    // in Celsius
  float f = dht.readTemperature(true); // in Fahrenheit

  // Check if readings failed
  if (isnan(h) || isnan(t) || isnan(f)) {
    Serial.println("Failed to read from DHT sensor!");
    return;
  }

  // Print values
  Serial.print("Humidity: ");
  Serial.print(h);
  Serial.print(" %\t");
  Serial.print("Temperature: ");
  Serial.print(t);
  Serial.print(" *C  ");
  Serial.print(f);
  Serial.println(" *F");

  // 🔥 Condition to turn on LED
  // Change the threshold as you want
  if (t > 30 && h > 60) {
    digitalWrite(LEDPIN, HIGH);
    Serial.println("🔥 LED ON - High temp & humidity!");
  } else {
    digitalWrite(LEDPIN, LOW);
    Serial.println("❄️ LED OFF - Normal conditions");
  }
}
```

---

### 🧠 Explanation

- `t > 30 && h > 60` → LED turns **ON** when temp > 30°C **and** humidity > 60%.  
  You can tweak these values for your use case (e.g. `t > 25` or `h < 40` etc.).
- `digitalWrite(LEDPIN, HIGH)` → turns LED **ON**.
- `digitalWrite(LEDPIN, LOW)` → turns LED **OFF**.
- `isnan()` check avoids reading errors from the DHT11.
- Fixed typos (`Serial.printIn` → `Serial.println`).

---

### ⚙️ Want it to react differently?
You can easily modify the condition logic, like:
- LED ON when **temperature only** exceeds threshold.  
  → `if (t > 30)`
- LED ON when **either** temperature or humidity is too high.  
  → `if (t > 30 || h > 60)`

